"In the first verse of the surah, it is stated that the disbelievers from among the People of the Book and the polytheists would not depart from their state of disbelief until a clear proof (bayyinah) came to them. This 'clear proof' is a Messenger sent by Allah. That Messenger recites purified pages, and within these pages are valuable, upright scriptures (the Quran).\n\nWhile the first verse states that 'they would not depart,' the fourth verse states, 'The People of the Book did not divide until after the clear proof had come to them.' There is an apparent contradiction between these two statements. Fakhr al-Din al-Razi relates five different interpretations to resolve this contradiction. The finest of these belongs to the author of al-Kashshaf: Before the Prophet came, those disbelievers had promised, 'We will not depart from our religion until he comes.' Allah first recounts this statement of theirs, and then states, 'The People of the Book did not depart' (that is, they did not keep their word). From another perspective, Elmalılı adds this: That era was an interregnum (fatrah); they were considered excused until the clear proof arrived. However, once the clear proof arrived, those who remained obstinate had no excuse left.\n\nThe fifth verse explains what was actually commanded to the People of the Book: to worship Allah alone, making religion purely sincere to Him as hanifs (those who turn away from false beliefs and incline toward truth), to perform the prayer, and to give zakah. This is the upright and steadfast religion (din al-qayyim). These are the fundamental principles brought by all prophets.\n\nIn the sixth verse, it is stated that the disbelievers who deny the clear proof after it has come will abide eternally in the fire of Hell. They are the worst of creatures.\n\nIn the seventh verse, those who believe and perform righteous deeds are characterized as the best of creation. Righteous deeds do not consist solely of obligatory duties such as prayer and zakah; they also encompass every good deed conforming to Allah's pleasure and avoiding the forbidden.\n\nIn the eighth verse, it is stated that the reward of these believers is the Gardens of Eden (Adn) beneath which rivers flow, where they will abide eternally. Furthermore, glad tidings are given that Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Allah. This immense reward and pleasure are exclusive to those who fear their Lord (having khashyah). Khashyah is proportional to knowledge and spiritual discernment (ma'rifah); scholars have a greater fear of Allah."